.TH BITWISE "1" "September 2026" "bitwise v0.70" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bitwise \- multi-base interactive calculator and bit manipulator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bitwise
[\fI\,OPTION\/\fR...] [\fI\,expression\/\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bitwise
is a multi-base interactive calculator supporting dynamic base conversion and
bit manipulation.  It is a handy tool for low level hackers, kernel developers
and device driver developers.
.PP
Given an expression on the command line, it evaluates the expression and prints
the result in every base at once.  Given no expression, it starts a full-screen
.BR ncurses (3)
interface in which a number can be edited in any base, and individual bits
toggled, with every other representation updating as you go.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interactive\fR
Load interactive mode (default if no input)
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-width\fR [\fBb\fR|\fBw\fR|\fBl\fR|\fBd\fR]
Set bit width (default: \fBl\fR): \fBb\fRyte (8 bit), \fBw\fRord (16 bit),
\fBl\fRong (32 bit) or \fBd\fRouble (64 bit).
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-si\fR
Print size according to SI standard. (default: IEC standard)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Start without color support
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Output version information and exit
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Display this help and exit
.SH COMMAND LINE MODE
In command line mode
.B bitwise
calculates the given expression and outputs the result in all bases, including
a labelled binary representation.
.PP
The base of the input is detected from its prefix:
.I 0x
or
.I 0X
for hexadecimal,
a leading
.I 0
for octal,
.I b
for binary, and anything else is decimal.  IPv4 addresses in dotted-quad form
are also accepted, and the result is reported in both network and reversed byte
order.
.PP
An expression starting with a minus sign would be taken for an option, so
separate it with \fB\-\-\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bitwise \-\- '\-5'
.fi
.RE
.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
.B bitwise
starts in interactive mode when no expression is given, or when
\fB\-i\fR is passed.  Type a number into any of the decimal, hexadecimal or
octal fields and the others follow, along with the binary row.  Press
.B F1
for the help screen.
.SS Navigation
.TP
.BR h ", " j ", " k ", " l ", arrow keys"
Move around.
.TP
.B space
Toggle the bit under the cursor.
.TP
.B w
Jump one byte forward.
.TP
.B b
Jump one byte backward.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.SS Operations
.TP
.B ~
Apply the NOT operator.
.TP
.B r
Reverse the endianness.
.TP
.BR < ", " >
Shift left or right.
.TP
.BR ! ", " @ ", " $ ", " *
Set the width to 8, 16, 32 or 64 bit respectively.  The number is
.I masked
with the new width, so reducing the width may lose precision.
.SH EXPRESSION CALCULATOR
Typing
.B :
in interactive mode enters the expression calculator, just as in
.BR vi (1).
.B ESC
leaves it.  Expressions typed here are evaluated, printed in the history
window, and shown in binary and in the various bases at the top.
.PP
The same expression syntax is used for expressions given on the command line.
.SS Operators
The syntax is a subset of C.  These operators are supported, in order of
precedence, tightest binding first:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
1   !  ~  \-  +     unary, right associative
2   *  /  %
3   +  \-
4   <<  >>
5   &
6   ^
7   |
.fi
.RE
.PP
Parentheses group as usual, and multiplication may be written implicitly:
.I 2(3)
and
.I (2)(3)
both give 6.  This is the same ordering a C compiler uses, so an expression
lifted out of source evaluates to the same thing here.
.PP
One difference from C:
.IR &= ,
.I ^=
and
.I |=
are accepted, but there is nothing to assign to, so they evaluate exactly like
.IR & ,
.I ^
and
.IR | .
.PP
Not supported: comparison and equality
.RI ( < ,
.IR > ,
.IR <= ,
.IR >= ,
.IR == ,
.IR != ),
logical
.I &&
and
.IR || ,
the ternary
.IR ?: ,
and exponentiation.
.SS Bits and the last result
.TP
.B $
The last result.
.TP
.BI BIT( x )
The value with bit
.I x
set.
.PP
So a bit can be set, cleared, toggled or tested with
.IR "$ |= BIT(n)" ,
.IR "$ &= ~BIT(n)" ,
.I "$ ^= BIT(n)"
and
.IR "$ & BIT(n)" .
.SS Commands
.TP
.BR h ", " help
Show the help screen.
.TP
.BR c ", " clear
Clear the history window.
.TP
\fBw\fR, \fBwidth\fR [\fB8\fR|\fB16\fR|\fB32\fR|\fB64\fR]
Set the required width mask.
.TP
\fBo\fR, \fBoutput\fR [\fBdec\fRimal|\fBhex\fRadecimal|\fBoct\fRal|\fBbin\fRary|\fBall\fR]
Set the default output for results.
.TP
.BR q ", " quit
Exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert a value and show every representation:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bitwise 0xdeadbeef
.fi
.RE
.PP
Evaluate a C-style expression:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bitwise "(1 << 31) | 0xff"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show an IPv4 address in both byte orders:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bitwise 192.168.1.1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Work on a byte, without color:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bitwise \-\-no\-color \-w b 0xff
.fi
.RE
.SH AUTHOR
Written by Ramon Fried.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
The full documentation for
.B bitwise
can be found at https://github.com/mellowcandle/bitwise
